# OOTD Collector Bot

Discord bot for archiving and reposting OOTD posts.

## Monthly Archive

Bot reposts one random OOTD from each day of a requested month into a new forum post.

The following channels must be configured by channel ID:

- Channel where OOTD images are grabbed from
- Channel where the forum post is announced
- Forum where the archive thread is posted
- Private bot command channel

### Usage

```text
!archive <month> <year>
```

Example:

```text
!archive january 2025
```

### Archive Behavior

- Command only works in the configured private bot channel, allowing access to be restricted.
- Has a limit on how many times a given month/year can be generated. Default is 3.
- Only posts to the configured forum channel.
- Can announce the newly generated archive in a separately configured channel.
- Can handle Needle bot auto-thread behavior.
- Selection is random and is not reaction based.
- No permanent local image storage is used.
- Images are retrieved from Discord, posted to the archive, and then discarded from memory.

---

## Weekly OOTD Repost

The bot automatically reposts OOTDs from the previous week, Monday through Sunday.

One OOTD is randomly selected from each day.

The weekly repost destination is configured using `WEEKLYPOST_CHANNEL_ID`.

The automatic weekly repost runs on Monday.

### Manual Weekly Repost

A weekly repost can also be triggered manually from the configured bot command channel:

```text
!weeklyrepost
```

This is useful for testing the weekly repost without waiting for the scheduled run.

---

## Yearly Best OOTDs

The bot can generate a yearly collection of the most-reacted-to OOTDs.

For each month:

1. All eligible OOTD posts are scanned.
2. Reaction counts from all emoji are added together.
3. The top 3 posts for that month are selected.
4. If a reaction tie affects the top 3, the post with more messages in its thread wins.
5. If the thread-message counts are also tied, the winner is selected randomly.

Only ties that can actually affect the monthly top 3 require thread-history checks.

The monthly finalists are then ranked across the entire year.

For a complete 12-month year, this produces a maximum of:

```text
3 posts x 12 months = 36 OOTDs
```

The highest-ranked images are posted in the main yearly post. Remaining images are placed in a thread attached to that post.

Only the first image from each selected OOTD post is used.

The yearly repost uses the same destination configured by:

```text
WEEKLYPOST_CHANNEL_ID
```

Configured excluded users are ignored.

### Usage

Either command can be used:

```text
!year 2025
```

or:

```text
!2025
```

The earliest supported year is 2023.

For 2023, only May through December are searched because the archive begins in May 2023.

For the current year, only months up through the current date are searched.

Future years are rejected automatically.

---

# Raspberry Pi Bot Service

The bot runs as a `systemd` service on the Raspberry Pi.

This means the bot:

- Starts automatically when the Raspberry Pi boots.
- Does not require a tmux session.
- Automatically restarts if the Python process fails.
- Can be manually stopped for maintenance or code changes.
- Writes Python/debug output to the system journal.

Service name:

```text
ootd-bot
```

Bot directory:

```text
/home/fealdere/Desktop/repos/scripts/ootd_bot
```

Python virtual environment:

```text
/home/fealdere/Desktop/repos/scripts/ootd_bot/venv
```

Main Python file:

```text
/home/fealdere/Desktop/repos/scripts/ootd_bot/ootd_collector.py
```

---

## Bot Service Commands

### Check whether the bot is running

```bash
sudo systemctl status ootd-bot
```

Look for:

```text
Active: active (running)
```

Press `q` to exit the status screen.

### Stop the bot

```bash
sudo systemctl stop ootd-bot
```

Use this before making substantial code changes.

A deliberate stop will keep the bot stopped until it is manually started again or the Raspberry Pi is rebooted.

### Start the bot

```bash
sudo systemctl start ootd-bot
```

### Restart the bot

```bash
sudo systemctl restart ootd-bot
```

Use this after changing Python code.

### Enable automatic startup

```bash
sudo systemctl enable ootd-bot
```

The service is currently configured to start automatically at boot.

### Disable automatic startup

```bash
sudo systemctl disable ootd-bot
```

This prevents automatic startup after future boots.

It does not necessarily stop the currently running bot.

To disable and stop it:

```bash
sudo systemctl disable ootd-bot
sudo systemctl stop ootd-bot
```

---

## Viewing Bot Logs

Python output is written to the system journal.

### Show the most recent 30 lines

```bash
sudo journalctl -u ootd-bot -n 30 --no-pager
```

### Watch the bot logs live

```bash
sudo journalctl -u ootd-bot -f
```

This behaves similarly to watching the old bot process inside tmux.

Press:

```text
Ctrl+C
```

to stop watching the logs.

### Show more history

```bash
sudo journalctl -u ootd-bot -n 100 --no-pager
```

### Show logs from the current boot

```bash
sudo journalctl -u ootd-bot -b --no-pager
```

A successful startup should contain output similar to:

```text
[DEBUG] Loading WeeklyRepostCog...
[DEBUG] WeeklyRepostCog initialized
[DEBUG] weekly_repost loop started
[DEBUG] Loading YearlyRepostCog...
[DEBUG] YearlyRepostCog initialized
[DEBUG] Waiting for bot ready...
[DEBUG] Bot ready, loop will begin
Logged in as ootd collector#4144
```

---

# Updating the Bot

The normal code-update procedure is:

### 1. Stop the bot

```bash
sudo systemctl stop ootd-bot
```

### 2. Edit the code

For example:

```bash
vim components/yearly_repost.py
```

or:

```bash
vim components/weekly_repost.py
```

or:

```bash
vim ootd_collector.py
```

### 3. Check Python syntax

From the bot directory:

```bash
python3 -m compileall -q -x 'venv' .
```

If this produces no output, the Python files passed the syntax check.

If there is a syntax error, fix it before restarting the bot.

### 4. Start the bot again

```bash
sudo systemctl start ootd-bot
```

### 5. Verify startup

```bash
sudo systemctl status ootd-bot
```

Then check the bot logs:

```bash
sudo journalctl -u ootd-bot -n 30 --no-pager
```

Look for:

```text
Logged in as ootd collector#4144
```

---

## Quick Update Workflow

For routine changes:

```bash
cd ~/Desktop/repos/scripts/ootd_bot

sudo systemctl stop ootd-bot

# Edit files
vim components/yearly_repost.py

# Check syntax
python3 -m compileall -q -x 'venv' .

# Start bot
sudo systemctl start ootd-bot

# Watch startup
sudo journalctl -u ootd-bot -f
```

If the bot is already running and the changes are simple, it can also just be restarted after editing:

```bash
sudo systemctl restart ootd-bot
```

---

# Editing the systemd Service

The service configuration is located at:

```text
/etc/systemd/system/ootd-bot.service
```

Edit it with:

```bash
sudo vim /etc/systemd/system/ootd-bot.service
```

After changing the service file, systemd must reload its configuration:

```bash
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
```

Then restart the bot:

```bash
sudo systemctl restart ootd-bot
```

Changes to normal Python files do NOT require `daemon-reload`.

Only changes to the systemd service file require it.

---

# Environment / Discord Token

The Discord token is stored separately from the Python source code.

Environment file:

```text
/home/fealdere/Desktop/repos/scripts/ootd_bot/.env
```

The file contains:

```text
OOTD_BOT_TOKEN=<token>
```

Do not commit this file to Git or share its contents.

Permissions should be restricted:

```bash
chmod 600 .env
```

Check permissions with:

```bash
ls -l .env
```

Expected permissions:

```text
-rw-------
```

The bot reads the token in Python using:

```python
os.getenv("OOTD_BOT_TOKEN")
```

The systemd service loads the `.env` file when starting the bot.

---

# Manual Bot Startup

The old manual startup command is available as:

```bash
runbot
```

`runbot` activates the bot virtual environment and starts `ootd_collector.py`.

Normally, do NOT use `runbot` while the systemd service is running.

Otherwise, two instances of the Discord bot may be running at the same time.

If manual startup is needed for debugging:

```bash
sudo systemctl stop ootd-bot
runbot
```

When finished, stop the manually running bot with:

```text
Ctrl+C
```

Then restore the systemd-managed bot:

```bash
sudo systemctl start ootd-bot
```

---

# Reboot / Power Loss

The bot is configured to start automatically after the Raspberry Pi boots.

Reboot:

```bash
sudo reboot
```

After reconnecting to the Pi, do NOT run `runbot`.

Check that systemd started the bot automatically:

```bash
sudo systemctl status ootd-bot
```

Then verify Discord login:

```bash
sudo journalctl -u ootd-bot -b --no-pager
```

Look for:

```text
Logged in as ootd collector#4144
```

If that appears after the reboot, the automatic startup completed successfully.

---

